 implies "wine owned by the Elves" at the time which is being considered, irrespective of the origin of the wine.
\par }{\i\fs28\lang1033\cgrid0 B.}{\fs28\lang1033\cgrid0  You have (/possess) a cup of gold.}{\i\lang1033\cgrid0  (}{\b\i\lang1033\cgrid0 Yulma maltava}{\i\lang1033\cgrid0  "cup of gold":  the possessive-adjectival case used in its "compositive" sense, de
noting what something is made of.)}{\i\fs28\lang1033\cgrid0 
\par C.}{\fs28\lang1033\cgrid0  The horse of the Elf [/the Elf's horse] has fallen into the deep gorge. }{\i\lang1033\cgrid0 (}{\b\i\lang1033\cgrid0 I rocco i Eldava}{\i\lang1033\cgrid0 
 "the Elf's horse": possessive case used of current ownership. One could argue that Tolkienian Elves seem to be so close to their horses that to them, their steeds are more like family members than possessions, and then it would be more appropriate to use
 the genitive case: }{\b\i\lang1033\cgrid0 i rocco i Eldo}{\i\lang1033\cgrid0  or }{\b\i\lang1033\cgrid0 i Eldo rocco}{\i\lang1033\cgrid0 . But as I said in the Introduction, the "Elves" of these exercises are not necessarily Tolkienian Elves.)}{
